Template: pubsec-fail2ban/api-url
Type: string
Default: http://127.0.0.1:8081
Description: URL of the pubsecd REST API:
 pubsec-fail2ban reports fail2ban bans to a pubsecd instance and reads the
 active list back from it. Enter the address of the internal REST API, which
 is the [api] bind address in the pubsecd configuration.

Template: pubsec-fail2ban/push-token
Type: password
Description: pubsecd API token with the "push" scope:
 This token is used to report bans observed by fail2ban. Create it in
 pubsec-tui on the Tokens tab; the secret is shown only once.
 .
 Leave empty to configure the token by hand later. Until both tokens are set,
 the service is enabled but will not start successfully.

Template: pubsec-fail2ban/read-token
Type: password
Description: pubsecd API token with the "read" scope:
 This token is used to fetch the active list that is banned locally. Its
 max_tlp setting limits which entries are handed out.
 .
 Leave empty to configure the token by hand later.

Template: pubsec-fail2ban/ban-active
Type: boolean
Default: true
Description: Ban all active IPs from the pubsecd active list?
 pubsec-fail2ban can write the addresses of the pubsecd active list into a log
 file that a dedicated fail2ban jail follows, so that threats reported by your
 peers are blocked locally before they reach this host.
 .
 Declining leaves the local jails reporting to pubsecd without importing any
 bans from it.

Template: pubsec-fail2ban/integrations
Type: multiselect
Choices: ${choices}
Description: fail2ban jails to report to pubsecd:
 Bans from the selected jails are published as observations. Jails that are
 already enabled on this system are preselected.
 .
 Entries starting with "pubsec-" are jails shipped by this package for Apache,
 nginx and HAProxy access logs; selecting one installs its jail configuration
 into /etc/fail2ban/jail.d. They complement the filters of the fail2ban
 package, which inspect the error logs instead.
 .
 The Apache and nginx jails expect the "combined" log format. Verify them with
 fail2ban-regex before relying on them.

Template: pubsec-fail2ban/haproxy-note
Type: note
Description: HAProxy jails read the systemd journal
 The selected HAProxy jails use the systemd journal as their backend and match
 on SYSLOG_IDENTIFIER=haproxy. If HAProxy on this host logs to a file instead,
 set a logpath in the corresponding file under /etc/fail2ban/jail.d.
